CAT = "phmwObstetrics"; SEG = "OBS"
# Obstetrics, with obstetrical anatomy and physiology (R.A. 7392 Sec. 12 b, d, e). 40 items, 10 per test.
# Kept clear of phnle's maternal items (Naegele, gravida/para, fundal height, Leopold's third
# maneuver, previa, PROM, preeclampsia room, magnesium antidote, TT spacing, lochia serosa,
# atony massage, anti-D timing, ectopic, mole, incompetent cervix, station +2, EINC drying).
Q = [
("Which hormone keeps the corpus luteum working in early pregnancy?",
 "hCG", ["Oxytocin", "Prolactin", "FSH"],
 "Human chorionic gonadotropin from the trophoblast keeps the corpus luteum producing progesterone until the placenta takes over. It is also the hormone that pregnancy tests detect."),
("In which part of the fallopian tube does fertilization normally take place?",
 "Ampulla", ["Isthmus", "Fimbriae", "Interstitial part"],
 "The ampulla, the wide outer third of the tube, is the usual site of fertilization. The fertilized ovum then travels to the uterus over several days."),
("A woman in her first pregnancy usually first feels fetal movement at about:",
 "18 to 20 weeks", ["10 to 12 weeks", "26 to 28 weeks", "6 to 8 weeks"],
 "Quickening is felt at about 18 to 20 weeks in a first pregnancy and a little earlier, about 16 to 18 weeks, by women who have felt it before."),
("Chadwick's sign of pregnancy is:",
 "Bluish cervix and vagina", ["Softening of the cervix", "Softened lower uterine segment", "Painless contractions"],
 "Chadwick's sign is the bluish-purple color from increased blood flow. A softened cervix is Goodell's sign and a softened lower uterine segment is Hegar's sign."),
("Which finding is a positive sign of pregnancy?",
 "Fetal heart tones heard", ["Missed period", "Positive urine pregnancy test", "Ballottement of the fetus"],
 "Positive signs come only from the fetus: fetal heart tones, fetal movements felt by the examiner, or the fetus seen on ultrasound. A urine test and ballottement are probable signs; a missed period is presumptive."),
("Which diameter of the fetal head presents when the head is well flexed in a vertex presentation?",
 "Suboccipitobregmatic", ["Occipitofrontal", "Occipitomental", "Submentobregmatic"],
 "Full flexion brings the smallest diameter, the suboccipitobregmatic, about 9.5 cm, through the pelvis. Submentobregmatic presents in a face presentation."),
("The true conjugate is estimated by subtracting how much from the diagonal conjugate?",
 "1.5 to 2 cm", ["3 to 4 cm", "0.5 cm", "5 cm"],
 "The diagonal conjugate is measured on internal examination, and 1.5 to 2 cm is subtracted for the height and slope of the symphysis to estimate the true (obstetric) conjugate."),
("Which pelvis has a round inlet and is the most favorable for vaginal birth?",
 "Gynecoid", ["Android", "Anthropoid", "Platypelloid"],
 "The gynecoid pelvis has a round inlet and wide subpubic arch. The heart-shaped android pelvis is the least favorable."),
("The relationship of the long axis of the fetus to the long axis of the mother is called:",
 "Lie", ["Attitude", "Position", "Presentation"],
 "Lie is longitudinal, transverse or oblique. Attitude is the flexion of the fetal parts; position relates the presenting part to the maternal pelvis."),
("In the cardinal movements of labor, which movement follows internal rotation?",
 "Extension", ["Descent", "External rotation", "Flexion"],
 "The order is engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation (restitution) and expulsion. The head extends as it passes under the pubic arch."),
("Which is a sign that the placenta has separated?",
 "The cord lengthens", ["The uterus flattens", "The fundus sinks lower", "Bleeding stops fully"],
 "Separation shows as a lengthening cord, a small gush of blood, and a uterus that becomes globular and rises in the abdomen. The uterus does not flatten or sink."),
("In active management of the third stage of labor, oxytocin is given:",
 "Within 1 minute of birth", ["After the placenta delivers", "As the head crowns", "30 minutes after birth"],
 "Oxytocin 10 IU IM is given within one minute of the baby's birth, after checking there is no second baby. Giving it only after the placenta loses most of its benefit."),
("Controlled cord traction is applied together with:",
 "Counter-pressure above the pubis", ["Steady pressure on the fundus", "A soft uterus between contractions", "The mother bearing down hard"],
 "One hand pulls gently on the cord during a contraction while the other pushes the uterus upward above the pubis. This guards against uterine inversion; fundal pressure is never used."),
("A placenta that is delivered with its shiny fetal surface first follows which mechanism?",
 "Schultze", ["Duncan", "Credé", "Brandt-Andrews"],
 "Schultze separation starts at the center, so the shiny fetal side appears first. Duncan separation starts at the edge, so the rough maternal side shows first."),
("A perineal tear that reaches the anal sphincter is classified as:",
 "Third degree", ["Second degree", "First degree", "Fourth degree"],
 "A third-degree tear involves the anal sphincter. A fourth-degree tear also goes through the rectal mucosa; a second-degree tear stops at the perineal muscles."),
("Which finding shows that a woman is in true labor?",
 "Progressive cervical dilation", ["Contractions ease on walking", "Irregular, unchanged intervals", "Discomfort in the lower abdomen only"],
 "Only true labor dilates and effaces the cervix. False labor contractions are irregular, felt in front and often ease with walking or rest."),
("On a partograph, the dilation line crosses to the right of the alert line. This means labor is:",
 "Progressing under 1 cm an hour", ["Moving faster than expected", "Already in the second stage", "Showing definite fetal distress"],
 "The alert line marks a rate of 1 cm per hour in the active phase. Crossing it means progress is slow, and the woman should be watched closely and referred if she reaches the action line."),
("Ovulation usually happens about how many days before the next menstrual period?",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "21 days", "28 days"],
 "The luteal phase is fairly constant at about 14 days, so ovulation falls about 14 days before the next period whatever the cycle length."),
("Where injectable oxytocin is not available, which drug can be given by mouth to prevent postpartum hemorrhage?",
 "Misoprostol", ["Carboprost", "Magnesium sulfate", "Nifedipine"],
 "Oral misoprostol is the recommended alternative uterotonic when oxytocin cannot be given. Carboprost is injected, magnesium sulfate prevents eclamptic seizures and nifedipine relaxes the uterus."),
("Bright red bleeding continues after delivery although the uterus is firm and well contracted. The likely cause is:",
 "A birth canal laceration", ["Uterine atony", "A distended full bladder", "Subinvolution of the uterus"],
 "Steady bleeding with a firm uterus points to a tear of the cervix, vagina or perineum, which needs to be found and repaired. Atony gives a soft, boggy uterus."),
("A woman at 36 weeks has dark vaginal bleeding, severe abdominal pain and a hard, tender uterus. This suggests:",
 "Abruptio placentae", ["Placenta previa", "Hydatidiform mole", "Normal show"],
 "Painful bleeding with a rigid, board-like uterus is typical of placental abruption. Placenta previa bleeds painlessly with a soft uterus."),
("Slight bleeding and cramping in early pregnancy with a closed cervix and no tissue passed is a:",
 "Threatened abortion", ["Inevitable abortion", "Incomplete abortion", "Missed abortion"],
 "In a threatened abortion the cervix stays closed and the pregnancy may continue. An open cervix makes it inevitable; passed tissue with some retained makes it incomplete."),
("A woman on magnesium sulfate for severe preeclampsia shows which early sign of toxicity?",
 "Absent knee-jerk reflex", ["Urine output of 50 mL an hour", "Breathing rate of 18", "Mild facial flushing"],
 "Loss of the patellar reflex is the first sign of rising magnesium levels; the dose is held and the doctor told. Urine output of 50 mL an hour and a rate of 18 are acceptable, and flushing is a common side effect."),
("Screening for gestational diabetes is usually done at:",
 "24 to 28 weeks", ["8 to 12 weeks", "34 to 36 weeks", "16 to 18 weeks"],
 "Insulin resistance from placental hormones rises in the second half of pregnancy, so routine screening is done at 24 to 28 weeks. Women at high risk may be tested earlier."),
("Why does hemoglobin fall slightly in normal pregnancy?",
 "Blood is diluted by plasma", ["Iron is lost in the urine", "The bone marrow slows down", "Red cells break down faster"],
 "Plasma volume rises by about half while red cell mass rises less, so the blood is diluted. This is physiologic anemia; true iron deficiency still needs treatment."),
("A woman at 35 weeks feels dizzy and faint while lying flat on her back. What should the midwife do?",
 "Turn her onto her left side", ["Raise the head of the bed fully", "Lower her head below her feet", "Keep her supine with legs raised"],
 "The heavy uterus presses on the inferior vena cava and cuts venous return. Lying on the left side relieves the pressure at once."),
("A pregnant woman wakes at night with a painful calf cramp. Which advice gives quick relief?",
 "Pull the toes upward", ["Point the toes downward", "Walk on tiptoe", "Bend the knee fully"],
 "Dorsiflexing the foot stretches the calf muscle and ends the cramp. Pointing the toes shortens the muscle and makes the cramp worse."),
("A ridge across the abdomen between the upper and lower uterine segments in obstructed labor warns of:",
 "Impending uterine rupture", ["Normal second stage", "Placental separation", "A full bladder"],
 "A pathological retraction ring (Bandl's ring) forms when the upper segment thickens and the lower segment thins over an obstruction. It signals that the uterus may rupture and the woman needs urgent referral."),
("Engagement means the biparietal diameter of the fetal head has passed through the:",
 "Pelvic inlet", ["Pelvic outlet", "Ischial tuberosities", "Vaginal opening"],
 "The head is engaged when its widest transverse diameter has entered the pelvic inlet, usually with the leading part at the level of the ischial spines, station 0."),
("Greenish amniotic fluid seen in labor shows that:",
 "Meconium has passed", ["The fluid is normal", "The placenta has separated", "The mother has diabetes"],
 "Green fluid means the fetus has passed meconium, which can be a sign of distress, so the fetal heart is watched closely and the baby assessed at birth. Normal fluid is clear or straw-colored."),
("Late decelerations of the fetal heart rate are a sign of:",
 "Placental insufficiency", ["Fetal head compression", "Umbilical cord compression", "A fetal sleep cycle"],
 "Late decelerations begin after the contraction peaks because the placenta cannot supply enough oxygen. Early decelerations come from head compression, variable ones from the cord."),
("On abdominal palpation, a hard, round, ballotable mass is felt in the fundus. The presentation is likely:",
 "Breech", ["Vertex", "Face", "Shoulder"],
 "A hard, round part that moves independently in the fundus is the fetal head, so the buttocks present. In a vertex presentation the soft, irregular breech lies in the fundus."),
("The puerperium lasts about:",
 "6 weeks", ["2 weeks", "3 months", "10 days"],
 "The puerperium runs from delivery of the placenta until the reproductive organs return close to their nonpregnant state, about 6 weeks."),
("Afterpains are usually strongest in which mother?",
 "A breastfeeding multipara", ["A bottle-feeding primipara", "A primipara with a small baby", "A primipara on bed rest"],
 "A uterus that has stretched before contracts and relaxes more, and suckling releases oxytocin, so afterpains are worst in multiparas who breastfeed."),
("On the fourth day after delivery a mother has a fever of 38.6 °C and foul-smelling lochia. This suggests:",
 "Puerperal infection", ["Normal involution", "Breast engorgement", "Urinary retention"],
 "Fever with foul lochia and a tender uterus points to endometritis, the most common puerperal infection. Normal lochia has a fleshy, not foul, smell."),
("A postpartum woman has calf pain when her foot is dorsiflexed. This suggests:",
 "Deep vein thrombosis", ["Leg muscle strain", "Calcium deficiency", "Varicose veins"],
 "Calf pain on dorsiflexion (Homans' sign) suggests thrombosis in the deep veins, which needs medical review. Pregnancy and the puerperium raise the risk of clotting."),
("Which previous operation puts a laboring woman at the highest risk of uterine rupture?",
 "Classical cesarean", ["Low transverse cesarean", "Appendectomy", "Dilation and curettage"],
 "A classical cesarean leaves a vertical scar in the thick upper segment, which can tear in labor. A low transverse scar ruptures far less often."),
("The umbilical cord prolapses out of the vagina in labor. Which position should the midwife place the woman in?",
 "Knee-chest position", ["Upright sitting position", "Walking about", "Flat on her back"],
 "Knee-chest or a steep head-down position lets the fetus fall away from the cord while help is called. The cord is never pushed back into the uterus."),
("Which maneuver delivers the aftercoming head in a breech birth?",
 "Mauriceau maneuver", ["McRoberts maneuver", "Ritgen maneuver", "Woods screw maneuver"],
 "In the Mauriceau-Smellie-Veit maneuver the baby lies on the forearm with a finger on the face to keep the head flexed while it is delivered. McRoberts and Woods are for shoulder dystocia."),
("The Ritgen maneuver is used during delivery to:",
 "Control the birth of the head", ["Free a cord around the neck", "Deliver the anterior shoulder", "Separate the placenta"],
 "Pressure on the perineum toward the chin while the other hand keeps the head flexed lets the head be born slowly, which helps prevent perineal tears."),
]
